A||N||6||Goatman||Head gasket||||1||
Z||000000||Goatman||05-11-2001||11:24 AM||||Can anyone tell me how are it will be to replace the head gasket on a '84 4 cyl. Thanks<P>if you can give me a vague description on how to do it that would be great too.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Goatman<BR>   Goatman||||reg||1
Z||000001||maryjane||05-11-2001||11:08 PM||||Except for all the bendin over, it,s not much different than any cyl hd. If you are going to use the old bolts, it's a good idea to put them in the same holes they came out of. You will have to remove all the attaching parts first. some of these include,dogbone,intake manifold,linkage for tbi,alternator & bracket,heater hose connection (can be a real pain)exh manifold,coil bracket. Unless you are real familiar with the vacum lines and electrical harness, mark EVERYTHING, so you can get it back together. It's a good practice to have head checked for cracks while you have it off.A Haynes manual will help some, but remember to take your time and maybe nothing will be broken when you go to reassemble it. I had hell, the first time I did mine, but the next time went pretty well. Good luck. There is a lot of help here if you need it,lots of little tricks you can learn. There is probably some info in search that will help,I haven't looked for it.<BR>Don & Jane||||reg||1
Z||000002||sqoach||05-12-2001||01:18 AM||||I've heard it's not a good idea to re-use the head bolts. Just go out and buy a new set. Shouldn't cost too much.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach2.JPG">   <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||1
Z||000003||MrPBody||05-12-2001||06:20 PM||||My story:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009419.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009419.html</A> <P>Definitely get a manual.  Sqoach is right about replacing head bolts.  Tighten them by torque-to-yield method (even though your manual describes a different procedure for '84.)<P>It could be a fight.  Good luck!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||sqoach||05-12-2001||08:32 PM||||Hey MrPBody, I have the same symptoms that you described in that thread. And I'm also having an overheating problem. I think it's really just a false overheat, but it's really looking like a head gasket leak.||||reg||1
Z||000005||PBJ||05-12-2001||08:42 PM||||If you are mechanically minded, have more spare time than cash, and a how to manual of some kind "you can do it"  worst case-broken<BR>head bolt(s), so you call a tow truck and send it to the garage and you can tell yourself you tried. Let a pro fix a broken head bolt.  Good luck-have fun. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||maryjane||05-12-2001||11:55 PM||||Your right about the head bolts, Thats why I had to do mine twice. Didn't know about the bolts when I did it the first time,a couple of weeks later, I read about it here and figured I better redo it before they broke. ||||reg||1
